INT. THE ROYAL OAK FUNCTION ROOM - WEDDING AFTERPARTY - NIGHT

Half-deflated balloons. A disco ball turns, one mirror missing. Somewhere a slow song limps to its end.

MO (50s, lanyard still around his neck under his dicky bow) holds a plastic cup of warm prosecco and has the look of a man cornered by a sofa. JEN (40s, heels in one hand) lowers herself into the chair opposite and says nothing for a good ten seconds.

JEN
Congratulations, by the way.

MO
Cheers. I'm not the groom.

JEN
No, but you did the seating plan.

MO
Technically, I did the spreadsheet.

Jen nods, slowly.

JEN
You sat Big Dave next to his ex.

MO
I wasn't told.

JEN
You were. In a meeting. Eleven times.

He opens his mouth. Closes it. Studies the carpet.

MO
Right.

A door SLAMS somewhere out in the car park. They both flinch.

JEN
That'll be Dave.

MO
Probably.

Jen sets her shoes on the table. She looks at them, and then at him, and something in her shoulders gives.

JEN
Honestly? It's the best wedding I've been to in years.

MO
Is it?

JEN
Mrs K cried at the vows. Dave cried at the buffet. Nobody's cried at work all year and I thought, there, that's a company away day.

He lets out a breath. Almost a laugh.

MO
I did write that in the risk assessment.

His phone BUZZES on the table. He reads it. Turns it face down.

JEN
Who's that?

MO
HR. They want a word about Monday.

JEN
Tell them it's a Saturday.

He smiles. She picks up her shoes. For the first time in eleven years, they stay for the last song.
